from pathlib import Path
import html, json
import build_course as _zh
import lang_switch

ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
EN=ROOT/'en'
def write(name,text):
    p=EN/name;p.parent.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)
    if p.suffix=='.html':text=lang_switch.inject(text,lang_switch.switch_link(p,ROOT))
    p.write_text(text,encoding='utf-8')
def esc(s):return html.escape(s)

# Folder slugs for the 14 units (translation spec section 4).
FOLDERS={'00':'00-art-fundamentals','01':'01-painting-and-printmaking','02':'02-calligraphy-and-seal-carving','03':'03-sculpture-and-installation','04':'04-architecture-and-landscape','05':'05-craft-and-design','06':'06-photography','07':'07-literature-and-narrative','08':'08-music-and-sound-art','09':'09-dance-and-body-based-art','10':'10-theatre-chinese-opera-and-live-performance','11':'11-film-animation-and-audiovisual-arts','12':'12-digital-media-and-game-art','13':'13-folk-art-and-intermedia'}

# Each row is an authored teaching unit, not an outline placeholder.
DATA=[
('00','Art Fundamentals','Art history, aesthetics, art criticism, curating and museum studies','Observe first, then interpret','visual',[
('Turn Feeling Into Evidence','Description points to positions, sounds, or movements that anyone can locate; interpretation is an inference about meaning; evaluation is a judgment made against standards. The three can support one another, but they must not be conflated. Saying "the picture feels lonely" is not enough: show how the spacing between the figures, the direction of the gazes, or the empty areas support that feeling.','A chair placed near the edge of the frame with a wide expanse of empty space around it: that is an observation, while "it suggests exclusion" is an interpretation. The emptiness might instead highlight the scale of the space, so the interpretation must leave other possibilities open.','Observe one object near you and write three descriptions, two interpretations, and one question to verify later.','What is an evidence-based interpretation?','Empty space that supports the sense of loneliness','The artist must have been lonely','Distinguish the visible evidence of the work from the artist\'s psychology; the latter requires documents.'),
('Return the Work to Its Society','Art history is not a relay race of styles. Materials, patrons, religion, trade, colonialism, and technologies of communication all shape how a work was produced and seen. Works close in date may have served entirely different lives.','Compare a ritual vessel with a commercial poster: both organize human attention, yet they address different occasions and audiences. They cannot be ranked by lifelikeness alone.','Find two works from different regions; for each, record the date, material, function, and collecting institution, writing "unknown" wherever something is unknown.','What does background research need first?','Verifying material and function','Guessing the creator\'s personality','Build a verifiable data card first; only then discuss social meaning.'),
('Write a Measured Review','A review can move in order through my question, the evidence in the work, background material, an alternative interpretation, and a tentative judgment. Tastes can differ while the argument remains discussable. Do not take one region\'s tradition as the measure of all art.','"I prefer quiet compositions" is a preference; "the repeated arrangement slows the movement of the eye" is a formal analysis others can discuss. Stating your standards makes the exchange more productive.','Write a 180-word review and ask a friend to point out the sentence least supported by evidence.','How do you improve a review?','Add an alternative interpretation','Delete all the open questions','Weighing competing interpretations tests whether your judgment is too strong.')]),
('01','Painting and Printmaking','Drawing, ink painting, oil painting, watercolor, mural, printmaking, illustration','Learn to see composition and materials','visual',[
('How the Eye Moves','Composition is the relationship among the elements of a picture. Size, placement, value, and repetition redirect attention. Simplifying the subject into masses and then describing the order of viewing is more reliable than rushing to guess the meaning of the subject matter.','The same red dot placed at the center and at the edge sets up different balances. A centered composition is not automatically solemn; the effect depends on the elements around it.','Reduce a photograph to three value masses; move one mass and compare where the eye lands first.','Which method best tests a composition?','Move a shape and compare','Recite the painter\'s birthday','Change only the position, and the difference made by position becomes easy to see.'),
('Color and Space','Hue distinguishes families of color, value distinguishes light from dark, and saturation distinguishes vividness; when the hues are arranged in a circle, the two colors opposite each other are complementary and contrast most strongly side by side. Space can be rendered by overlapping, size, perspective, or layering; one-point perspective must not be treated as the only correct way of depicting space.','An ink shanshui (landscape) painting can organize space through overlapping mountains and a path for the eye, an experience different from a perspective drawing at a fixed viewpoint.','With a single brush, paint three ranges of mountains, changing only the overlaps and the density, without any help from color.','What clue does overlapping mainly supply?','Which objects stand in front of which','The artist\'s childhood','A foreground covering a background is a spatial clue; it does not directly document the artist\'s life.'),
('Materials Speak Too','The covering power of pigment, the absorbency of paper, and the transfer of printing each leave different traces. A print usually produces its image through the relation between block and paper and cannot simply be read as a reproduction of a painting.','Transparent layering in watercolor and the covering power of thickly applied paint meet different limits when changes are made; a limit can also become a resource for style.','Draw one tree each with the side of a pencil, with the point, and by rubbing onto paper, and compare the traces.','What should a discussion of materials focus on?','How the marks were produced','The higher the price, the better the work','Material analysis attends to process and effect; price is not sufficient evidence of artistic quality.')]),
('02','Calligraphy and Seal Carving','Seal script, clerical script, regular script, running script, cursive script, pen calligraphy, seal carving','From reading characters to seeing lines','line',[
('Lines Keep Time','Beyond its textual content, calligraphy can be watched for entries and exits, turns, thickness, and the breaks between strokes. A line is the trace left by movement, yet lines alone cannot precisely recover the writer\'s mood.','The same character shan ("mountain"), outlined slowly or written in one pass, leaves different turns and rhythms; neatness is not the only standard of judgment.','Write the character for "mountain" three times with an ordinary pen, controlling speed, pressure, and size in turn and changing only one variable at a time.','What can a line prove directly?','Visible changes in thickness','The writer\'s mood that day','Psychological inference needs corroboration; changes in thickness can be observed directly.'),
('Character Structure and Overall Composition','Character structure studies the organization inside a single character; overall composition studies the relations among characters, columns, and empty space across the whole work. Seal, clerical, regular, running, and cursive are the customary script categories, and their development was no straight line in which each old style was wholly replaced by a new one. Identification can begin with features: seal script is even and elongated; clerical script carries the wavelike flaring stroke ("silkworm head, swallow tail"); regular script is upright and square; running script links its strokes; cursive script abbreviates into signs.','With the Palace Museum\'s material on Zhao Mengfu\'s running script, look first at the distance between characters, then at the balance of each column; you need not identify every character before you begin to look.','Arrange four characters in a loose version and a dense version, and explain which version slows the pace of viewing.','What does overall composition mainly examine?','Characters, columns, and space across the whole work','The translation of a single word','Overall composition looks at the organization of the whole; character structure looks at the single character.'),
('Positive and Negative on the Seal Face','Seal carving involves script, knife work, material, and the layout of the seal face. Zhuwen (relief) usually presents red characters; baiwen (intaglio) usually presents white characters; the organization of red and white, together with the border and any breaks, shapes what we see.','Within one square, keeping the red lines or cutting them away reverses the relation between characters and ground. An on-screen simulation is no substitute for a real stamped impression.','Draw two seal faces of your initials on paper, one in red characters and one in white, using no knife.','What does a baiwen (intaglio) seal usually present?','White characters on a red ground','Red characters on a white ground','In baiwen the characters usually appear in white against the red seal face.')]),
('03','Sculpture and Installation','Sculpture in the round, relief, public sculpture, readymade, site-specific installation, sound installation','Understand space through the body','space',[
('Walk Around the Work','Sculpture has volume, and the viewing position changes its silhouette, its overlaps, and its sense of balance. Relief depends on a backing plane; sculpture in the round usually allows viewing from many directions, though the actual display may limit the route.','A chair looks symmetrical from the front yet shows its tilt and supports from the side. A photograph of a three-dimensional work gives a single viewpoint.','Fold paper into a form that stands and draw its silhouette from three angles.','What is the better way to grasp volume?','Compare several observation points','Rely on one frontal photograph','A change of viewpoint reveals relationships that a single image conceals.'),
('Material and Scale','Stone, wood, metal, fiber, and everyday objects differ in weight, touch, and conditions of making. The relation between the work\'s scale and the human body also shapes feelings of intimacy, pressure, or monumentality.','The same geometric form held in the palm and raised above head height changes the experience of the body; the monumental is not automatically profound.','Photograph a paper model once beside a cup and once beside a toy figure, and record the feeling of scale.','What must an analysis of scale compare?','The relation between the work and the human body','How well known the artist is','The body provides the reference for scale; fame does not account for spatial experience.'),
('The Site Is Also a Medium','An installation often organizes the relations among objects, space, light, or sound, and the visitor\'s route may become part of the experience of the work. Setting out many objects does not by itself make an effective installation.','Chairs lined up as a waiting queue or arranged in a circle for discussion: the objects are unchanged, but the implied behavior has changed. This is a teaching experiment, not a historical work.','Use three objects to organize a space of "waiting" and write down how visitors would enter it.','What should a site analysis record first?','Routes and their relation to the setting','The purchase price of the objects','Site, route, and the relations among objects can support an analysis of meaning.')]),
('04','Architecture and Landscape','Architecture, interior space, garden, landscape, urban space, heritage conservation','How space arranges life','space',[
('Reading Plans and Circulation','A plan cuts space as seen from above; circulation is the route people follow through it. Entrances, turns, openings, and thresholds shape use, and a handsome facade is no substitute for the experience of the space.','In rooms of the same size, a door set in the middle or in a corner changes the paths of movement and the arrangement of furniture.','Draw a simple plan of one room in your home, marking doors, windows, and the route you walk most often.','What does circulation mean?','The routes people take through space','The color of the outer walls','Circulation concerns the paths of movement.'),
('Light and Structure','Columns, walls, and beams can carry loads and can also organize the rhythm of space. Structural systems follow two basic strategies: with load-bearing walls, openings are restricted; with a column-and-beam frame, the loads pass into the skeleton and the walls are set free. Arches and domes use curves to carry the weight to supports on either side, while trusses and suspension cables span wide distances through the tension and compression of their members. Natural light changes with the hour, and material surfaces alter its reflection and its feel. Here we only practice appreciation; no judgment of structural safety is being made.','A window cut in the side of a paper box and a hole cut in its top distribute light differently; this helps in understanding how light shapes space.','Cut two openings at different places in a paper box and compare the reach of the light with a flashlight.','How should one study light in space?','Compare times of day and openings','Record only the ticket price','The source of light and its change over time are observable variables.'),
('Gardens and Public Life','Landscape comprises landform, water, plants, paths, and human activity. A garden is not mere decoration, and urban space raises the question of who may enter, linger, and use it. Different traditions cannot be sorted simply into the natural and the artificial.','A plaza without seats and a plaza with shaded benches may affect lingering in different ways; this needs to be checked by observation on site.','Walk a familiar public route and note three places where one can linger and one obstacle to use.','What else should an evaluation of public space consider?','Whether different people can use it','Whether it looks grand enough in photographs','Accessibility and the experience of use are also dimensions of evaluation.')]),
('05','Craft and Design','Ceramics, metalwork, lacquer, weaving and embroidery, glass, furniture, fashion, graphic design, product design, interaction design','See form in use','visual',[
('Form and Use','Craft attends to material, skill, and traditions of making; design usually addresses problems of use and communication within a particular situation. The two overlap heavily and are not a simple opposition of hand and machine.','A cup\'s handle shapes both the grip and the silhouette; decoration alone cannot tell you whether it works well in the hand.','Choose two cups and record one difference each in grip, cleaning, weight, and appearance.','What does an evaluation of design require?','A clearly defined context of use','Counting the amount of decoration','The user and the task determine which standards are relevant.'),
('From Material to Process','Forming, joining, firing, dyeing, and weaving all shape the final form. Observing seams, texture, and repeated traces can suggest hypotheses about how a thing was made; confirming the process still requires the museum\'s documentation.','A repeated pattern may come from a mold or from repeated handwork; the eye alone cannot always settle the mode of production.','Find a textile or a ceramic, draw its seams and texture, and list one question about the process that still needs verification.','What should observation of process avoid?','Mistaking conjecture for fact','Recording the position of seams','Record visible traces and processes still to be checked separately.'),
('Visual Hierarchy and Responsibility','Type size, spacing, contrast, and arrangement guide the order of reading. Design must also weigh legibility, maintenance, the consumption of resources, and the people left out.','If the decoration of an event poster outshines its date and place, it may fail at giving notice however handsome it is.','Make two paper versions of an event notice and ask someone to find the time and place within five seconds.','How can you test whether a notice works?','Ask someone to find the key information','Just count how many colors were used','A task test checks directly whether the communication succeeded.')]),
('06','Photography','Documentary, portrait, landscape, still life, conceptual, experimental photography','See the choices behind the frame','visual',[
('Inside and Outside the Frame','Framing in photography chooses what to include and what to leave out. The image records light from the real world, but the moment chosen, the viewpoint, and the context of circulation also take part in making meaning; focal length sets the angle of view — wide-angle exaggerates depth and telephoto compresses space — so "near and far" is itself a technical choice.','The same table, cropped to remove the clutter, may look minimalist; that is not the whole truth of the scene.','Photograph one scene with a phone in wide, medium, and close framings, and write down what each leaves out.','What does the frame of a photograph mean?','A chosen part of the scene is presented','The complete facts of the scene','The evidentiary value of a photograph should be read together with its framing and its context.'),
('Time and Light','Exposure is set by three controls together: the shutter governs how long light enters and how movement is traced; the aperture governs the size of the opening and depth of field; ISO governs sensitivity and noise. Changing one can be offset with the other two, but each control leaves its side effects in the picture. The direction of light shapes shadow and texture, and no single parameter works as a universal formula for style.','Side light often makes surface relief more visible, while frontal light may weaken the differences of shadow; the exact effect needs to be compared on the same object.','Photograph one cup in window light, turning the cup or moving the camera, and compare the shadows.','What is best held constant when comparing light?','Keep the same subject','Change all the conditions continually','Hold the other conditions steady, so the effect of light can be isolated.'),
('Captions and Ethics','A caption states the people, place, time, and circumstances of the shot, and so helps limit misreading. Photographing and displaying raise consent, privacy, and power; a documentary photograph must not turn an unknown story into fact.','A person with head lowered may be grieving or reading a phone; "lowered head" is observable, "despair" needs further grounds.','Write one factual caption for a photograph of your own, then write your interpretation separately.','How should a factual caption handle the unknown?','State plainly what remains unverified','Invent a moving story','Marking the unknown clearly keeps image analysis credible.')]),
('07','Literature and Narrative','Poetry, fiction, essays, dramatic literature, oral literature, children\'s literature','From language to experience','text',[
('Imagery and Rhythm','Imagery evokes experience through concrete sensory detail; rhythm comes from sound, sentence length, pause, and repetition. Literature is not merely a translatable message hidden inside sentences.','"The cup on the windowsill has gone cold" offers more sensory clues than "time has passed." Both can work; the choice depends on the purpose of the writing.','Rewrite "I miss home" as three concrete details that do not name the feeling directly.','Which analysis stays closer to the language?','Pointing out repetition and pause','Guessing the author\'s zodiac sign','Textual analysis looks first for evidence in the words.'),
('Who Is Telling the Story','The narrator is the voice that tells the story and is not the same person as the author. Point of view decides what the reader can know; a first-person narrator may be sincere and may also misread events.','"I saw him put the letter away" gives an action yet proves nothing about what he was thinking. Limited information creates suspense.','Write the same farewell twice, 80 words each, once as a bystander and once as the person involved, and mark what each one knows.','Who is the first-person narrator necessarily?','The telling voice within the work','The author of the novel in person','The author creates the narrator; the two cannot simply be equated.'),
('Close Reading and Comparison','Close reading starts from words, syntax, and structure; comparison defines a shared question. A translation carries the translator\'s choices, so cross-language comparison should state the edition and avoid taking a translated line as the voice of the original.','Comparing two poems about rain, ask whether the rain is background, an obstacle to action, or a trigger of feeling, rather than only which poem is more beautiful.','Choose a classical poem you know well, circle its verbs, and explain what would be lost if one of them were replaced.','What must be settled before comparing works?','The question the comparison rests on','Which author is better known','A shared question gives the comparison a basis that can be discussed.')]),
('08','Music and Sound Art','Vocal music, instrumental music, composition, folk music, classical, jazz, popular, electronic, experimental sound','Learn to listen with purpose','rhythm',[
('Meter and Rhythm','A steady pulse serves as a reference in time; rhythm is the organization of long and short among sounds and rests. Tempo affects the spacing of the pulse; a rhythmic pattern can keep the same relations at different tempos.','Clap four times in a row, then replace the second clap with a rest: the underlying pulse can continue while the actual arrangement of sounds has changed.','Practice with the beat tool on the page: clap on every beat, then on every other beat, counting the beats steadily as you go.','What does a change of tempo alter first?','The interval between beats','The material of the instrument','Tempo changes the intervals of time; it does not change what the instrument is made of.'),
('Melody and Timbre','In a melody you can follow the direction of pitch, its repetitions, and the ends of phrases; timbre helps distinguish how a sound is produced. Sketch the contour of the melody first — listening can begin without reading notation. Loudness and pitch are not the same dimension.','The same melody sung by a voice and played on a flute can keep a similar contour while differing in the texture of the sound.','Hum a melody you know, draw its contour with rising and falling lines, then change the timbre of your hum.','What is the relation between pitch and loudness?','Two dimensions that can be told apart','High notes are necessarily louder','High-low and loud-soft should be listened for separately.'),
('Repetition, Change, and Listening Contexts','Structure can be heard through repetition, contrast, and return. Labels like A and B are tools of analysis, not a template that all music must fit. Music also works differently in ritual, in dance, and in playback from a recording.','When a passage returns, it can keep its melody while changing its orchestration; hearing a return does not mean every detail is the same.','Choose a song you know, draw a timeline of its sections, and mark one repetition and one change.','How should the return of a section be judged?','Compare what is similar and what has changed','Judge by the title of the song alone','A return is heard through relations among the musical material.')]),
('09','Dance and Body-Based Art','Chinese classical dance, folk and ethnic dance, ballet, modern dance, contemporary dance, street dance, choreography','How movement becomes expression','motion',[
('Body, Space, and Time','Dance can be observed through body parts, movement, space, time, and effort. Record what happened first and discuss emotion afterwards; no single movement carries one fixed meaning across all cultures.','An extended hand can invite, block, or search; direction, speed, and the distance from others change how it reads.','Seated, extend and withdraw your hand, once slowly and once suddenly, and record the difference in what you feel.','What should be recorded first in dance analysis?','The direction, speed, and effort of the movement','How sad the dancer must have been','Observe the movement first; propose emotional interpretations afterwards.'),
('Movement Relations and Choreography','Unison, taking turns, repetition, and contrast build relations within a group. Technique bears on expression, but difficulty is not the only standard of value.','Two dancers raise their arms in unison, then offset them by one beat, and the visual relation turns from sameness into echo.','Let two fingers stand for two dancers and perform one short phrase in two versions: in unison and in alternation.','What does alternating movement change most directly?','The timing between movements','The material the costumes are made of','Choreography changes the order of movements and their echoes.'),
('Culture and Watching','Dance may take part in ritual, in sociability, on the stage, or in the expression of identity. When watching different traditions, consult the accounts of performers and communities and avoid treating an unfamiliar movement as a token of the exotic.','The same step in a community celebration and on a theater stage may differ in how people join in and how closely they watch.','Watch a dance published by an established institution and record the occasion, the participants, and three pieces of movement evidence.','How should cross-cultural watching proceed?','Learn the community and the occasion first','Rank what you see by familiarity','Context helps explain what the movement means in its own setting.')]),
('10','Theatre, Chinese Opera and Live Performance','Spoken drama, xiqu (Chinese opera), opera, musical theatre, puppetry, quyi (narrative telling and singing), acrobatics, performance art','Read action and convention','text',[
('What Does the Character Want','Dramatic action can be observed through a character\'s goal, obstacle, and tactics. Beyond the spoken lines, pauses, blocking, and responses also carry the relations between people. A character is not reducible to a single personality label.','"Stay" can be a plea or a command; the other person\'s reaction makes the relation clearer.','Say "open the door" as a request, as a threat, and as a probe, and compare the pauses.','What question can open an analysis of action?','What the character wants to achieve right now','What the actor likes in daily life','Goal and obstacle are an effective entry into stage action.'),
('The Shared Conventions of the Stage','Performance and audience rely on conventions to understand the fiction. The conventions, the stylized action, and the role categories of xiqu each have their own traditions and cannot be judged only by whether they imitate daily life.','Without a real horse, the actor can still make the audience understand riding; the understanding comes from performance signs and the experience of watching.','Mime carrying a brimming bowl of water with empty hands, ask someone to guess, and discuss which movements helped.','What does understanding stylized performance require?','Understanding the conventions of movement signs','Looking only at how costly the props are','Through conventions, performance can convey situations that are never physically presented.'),
('The Stage Is a Collaborative Art','Director, actors, stage design, lighting, costume, and sound organize the watching together. The words of the script and a particular performance are not the same object; comparing performances should record the production version.','A dimming of the lights and a long pause before one line can shift the audience\'s expectations, but the meaning still depends on what comes before and after.','Write two versions of lighting and blocking notes for a two-line exchange and explain the relationship you mean to change.','What must be recorded when comparing performances?','The production version of each performance','Only the place named in the story','The production version helps distinguish different stage treatments.')]),
('11','Film, Animation and Audiovisual Arts','Fiction film, documentary, experimental film, animation, film and television, short film, video art','See how shots connect','sequence',[
('The Shot Selects Information','Shot scale controls the proportion of figure to surroundings; camera movements such as zoom, dolly, pan, tracking, and following, together with the angle, change the viewing position and the pace. The shot is a unit that selects information, and its work cannot be summed up as "looking good."','A long shot lets us see the figure in relation to its surroundings; a close shot can emphasize a face or an object; neither carries a fixed emotional effect.','Film the same cup in a long shot and a close shot and state what information each one supplies.','What does a change of shot scale directly affect?','The range of information inside the frame','The opinion the director must hold','Shot scale changes the range of information; meaning still depends on context.'),
('Editing Creates Relations','Editing organizes understanding through order, duration, and juxtaposition. The same expression cut to different objects can lead toward different inferences; what the audience infers is not the character\'s actual mind.','"Face - food" and "face - empty chair" can lead the same expression to be read differently. This is a teaching illustration.','Use the page to swap the order of the shots, write a one-sentence story, then state the inference it relies on.','What can the order of shots change?','The audience\'s inference about relations','The original size of the source files','Editing organizes meaning; it does not by itself alter the raw material.'),
('Sound and Animated Time','Onscreen (diegetic) sound has a visible source in the frame; the source of offscreen sound is not shown; sounds inside and outside the story world must also be told apart. Animation produces the experience of movement through the timing of successive images.','An empty corridor with footsteps makes the viewer expect someone to approach; sound can supply information the picture never shows.','Design two sound treatments for a ten-second static scene and state what each leads the viewer to expect.','What else does sound analysis need to consider?','The relation of the sound source to the story world','Only the number of colors in the frame','Whether a source is visible and whether it belongs to the story world are different questions.')]),
('12','Digital Media and Game Art','Interactive art, generative art, net art, games, virtual reality, augmented reality, AI art','Rules, too, can be a medium','interactive',[
('What Does Interaction Change','An interactive work receives input from participants and produces feedback through rules. A button is not in itself an artistic meaning; what matters is how input, rules, and experience are connected.','The slider on this page changes the spacing of the shapes, and the participant feels the shift between sparse and dense. It demonstrates a rule; it does not stand for a finished work of art.','Design a work that uses a single button: write down the input, the rule, the feedback, and the theme.','What is the focus of interaction analysis?','The relation of input, rule, and feedback','How many colors the button uses','The meaning of interaction comes from the link between action and feedback.'),
('Rules and Choices in Games','Games shape action through rules, goals, resources, and feedback. Narrative can come from words and images, and equally from the player\'s choices and their consequences in the system.','If every act of help consumes your own time, the trade-offs facing the player can become part of the experience; one must still check whether the rules truly allow a choice.','Design a three-round paper game that fixes one resource and two uses for it; playtest, then revise.','What shows whether a choice is meaningful?','Whether the consequences differ','Whether the menu text is long enough','Different choices must produce a difference that can be felt in experience or outcome.'),
('Generative Systems and Authorial Responsibility','Generative art uses rules to produce many outcomes. When working with AI, record the tool, the inputs, the selection, and the revisions, and discuss sources, rights, and bias in the data. Randomness does not mean the absence of authorial choice.','Setting a range of colors, a quantity, and criteria for rejection already shapes the generated result; the look of an image alone cannot confirm where its data came from.','Write a rule that lets a die decide the direction of a line, produce three results, and record which one you kept and why.','What should be recorded when discussing a generative work?','The rules, the selection, and the revisions','Only the size of the final image','A record of process helps analyze the author\'s choices and the system\'s role.')]),
('13','Folk Art and Intermedia','Paper cutting, New Year prints (nianhua), shadow puppetry, folk narrative, festive arts, performance art, intermedia practices','Understand art in relation to life','visual',[
('Works and Their Occasions in Life','Folk art is usually tied to local life, the seasons, belief, the family, or a trade. "Folk" does not mean anonymous, ancient, or unchanging; attend to the particular makers and communities.','The same paper cutting on a window, on a ritual object, and in an art museum faces different viewers, and its function may change as well.','Ask a family member how one festive object is used, and separate what they have seen from what they have heard.','What matters most in documenting folk art?','The particular community and occasion of use','Calling it an eternal tradition','Concrete times, places, and users guard against empty generalization.'),
('Silhouettes, Narrative, and Collaboration','Forms such as shadow puppetry can combine figural design, manipulation, music, and telling. Sorting by material is convenient, yet it can cut across the intermedia experience the work actually offers.','The paper shape is only one part of the shadow play; the rhythm of the manipulation and the telling decide whether the character comes alive.','Cut a simple silhouette from paper, move it before a flashlight, and add two lines of narration.','What else must be observed to understand a shadow play?','How manipulation, sound, and narrative relate','Counting how many colors the paper uses','Several media together form the experience of the performance.'),
('Crossing Boundaries and Grounded Judgment','Performance and intermedia art may take the body, an event, a text, or an institution itself as material. Judgment need not stop at "is this even art"; one can ask about the rules, the context, the audience\'s experience, and the question the work poses.','Turning an everyday queue into an organized, announced event changes the frame of watching; whether it has value still needs concrete analysis.','Design a one-minute "waiting" piece that disturbs no one, stating the rules and the question you want the audience to think about.','Where can criticism of an intermedia work begin?','Check its rules and context first','Dismiss it out of unfamiliarity','Only by understanding how a work operates can criticism become specific.')])]

